cursor-agent --resume e53e6dba-33d8-48b5-bb30-2c8a1e18b7f2 --model gemini-4-argon-medium --print --force --trust --output-format stream-json $'<instructions>\nGenerate a single self-contained HTML file that runs directly in a browser.\n\nBuild a photoreal, full-3D lava lamp. A classic tapered lava lamp stands on a surface in a dim room. Inside the glass, a bulb heats colored wax suspended in a tinted liquid. Wax blobs swell at the bottom, detach, rise, stretch, merge, split, cool near the top, and sink back down. It must feel like a real object you could stare at for minutes, not a looping animation or a flat 2D effect.\n</instructions>\n\n<core-experience>\nThe page is one fullscreen 3D scene centered on the lamp.\n\n- The lamp is the hero. It is lit, running, and visibly moving on first load. Do not require a click before the wax moves.\n- The user can orbit around the lamp, zoom within sensible limits, and see it from any side. The motion and lighting must hold up from every angle, including close-up.\n- The lamp is the main light source of the scene. Its glow spills onto the surface it stands on and the nearby room, and that light flickers and shifts as wax passes in front of the bulb.\n- Wax motion is continuous and never visibly repeats. Blobs vary in size, speed, and shape over time.\n\nThe result should feel like a premium product shot of a real lava lamp, filmed at night.\n</core-experience>\n\n<lamp-object>\nModel a recognizable lava lamp, not a capsule or a cylinder.\n\n- A tapered glass vessel, wider at the bottom, with real thickness, a rounded top, and a sealed bottom.\n- A conical metal base that hides the bulb, and a matching metal cap. Brushed or polished metal with visible reflections.\n- A visible power cord leading off the surface, and a small physical switch on the base or cord.\n- Proportions and scale must read as a real tabletop object.\n</lamp-object>\n\n<wax-and-liquid>\nThe wax is the core of the experience.\n\n- The wax is a continuous, merging fluid volume. Blobs must smoothly fuse when they touch and neck apart when they separate. Separate spheres that overlap or pop are not acceptable.\n- Motion is driven by a heat model: wax near the bulb warms, expands, and rises; wax near the cap cools, contracts, and sinks. A pool of warm wax stays at the bottom and feeds new blobs.\n- Rising blobs stretch vertically and wobble. Small droplets can pinch off from larger ones. Falling blobs are slower and denser looking.\n- Blobs stay inside the glass. They follow the taper of the vessel and never clip through the walls, cap, or base.\n- The wax looks translucent and warm, brighter where it is thin and near the bulb, darker and more saturated where it is thick. A subsurface-scattering look is expected.\n- The liquid is tinted, slightly hazy, and refracts what is behind it. The wax color and liquid color must contrast.\n</wax-and-liquid>\n\n<controls>\nProvide a small, elegant control panel that does not cover the lamp.\n\n- Power switch. Turning the lamp off fades the bulb and the room light, and the wax gradually slows, cools, and settles into a pool at the bottom. Turning it back on warms the wax first; the first blob takes a few seconds to form and rise, like a real lamp.\n- At least four color presets that change both the wax and the liquid, including a classic red wax in yellow liquid.\n- Heat or flow speed.\n- Wax amount.\n- Optional: room light level, slow motion, an auto-orbit camera.\n\nEvery control must visibly change the scene in real time.\n</controls>\n\n<rendering>\nPush the rendering as far as a single HTML file allows while staying smooth.\n\n- Three.js, loaded from a pinned CDN version. three/addons from that same version are allowed.\n- The wax may be rendered with raymarched signed distance fields, marching cubes, or any technique that produces a smooth merging surface. It must be properly composited with the glass and the scene.\n- Physically based glass: refraction or transmission, Fresnel reflections, visible thickness, and specular highlights from the room.\n- Physically based metal on the base and cap with environment reflections. Generate the environment in code or with Three.js RoomEnvironment. Do not depend on an external HDR file.\n- The wax and the bulb glow. Use bloom or an equivalent so the glow reads as light, not as a flat color.\n- The lamp casts light and soft shadows onto the surface. The surface itself has a believable material such as wood, stone, or fabric.\n- Tone mapping (ACES or filmic) is required. Subtle film grain, vignette, or depth of field are allowed if they improve the shot.\n- A dim but readable room: a table or shelf surface, a wall, and a few simple props are enough to give scale and catch the light.\n</rendering>\n\n<performance>\nThe page must stay smooth on a modern laptop.\n\n- Target a stable high frame rate at full-window resolution. Use a resolution scale or adaptive quality for expensive passes if needed.\n- Keep raymarch step counts, blob counts, and post passes bounded.\n- Advance the wax simulation with a fixed timestep so a frame hitch cannot make blobs explode or escape the glass.\n</performance>\n\n<output-format>\nOne HTML file only. No extra JS, CSS, images, models, or texture files. No package manager and no dev server. Three.js may load from a CDN. Everything else, including shaders and procedural textures, is inline.\n\nThe file must start with `<!DOCTYPE html>` and end with `</html>`.\n</output-format>\n\n<important>\n- You MUST write the result directly into a file named "index.html" in the current working directory.\n- The page `<title>` must be your model name.\n- The only visible title or brand in the page must also be your model name. Add nothing else to either title.\n- Do not ask clarifying questions. Make the design and engineering decisions yourself.\n</important>\n\nWork only inside the current working directory. Do not read, list, search, or edit files outside the current working directory. Treat it as a standalone project root.\n\nUse pnpm exclusively if dependency installation or package scripts are needed. Do not use npm.\nSet the browser title and the primary visible title to your model name, using only identity information already available to you. Add nothing else to either title.\nRun the relevant checks yourself. Do not edit files outside the current working directory.' 
